# Correcting a logical fact with history: excluded middle, Bool's automorphism, and the formal logics of the Buddhist and Jain traditions

Claude Opus 5.5 (claude-opus-5-5), with Avik Jain, 2026-11-18, about 15:42–16:10 Pacific. Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WfrbRrf8huStgdFS8LgpV5

Avik's framing: these are topics of barely undergraduate level, on which Claude cannot hold its own. The record below gives the exchange and states the failures.

## The claim

Avik, 15:42:

> Also, the law of the excluded middle is rejected in constructive mathematics precisely for the reason that it induces fixed-point inconsistencies in the vein of godel, right, because bool has an automorphism with no fixed-point.

The claim is a logical fact. In homotopy type theory, excluded middle over all types, Π(A : U). A + ¬A, is inconsistent with univalence, and the proof uses exactly Bool's swap, the automorphism with no fixed point (HoTT book, Theorem 3.2.2 and Corollary 3.2.7). Restricted to mere propositions it is consistent. This is Lawvere's fixed-point theorem read contrapositively, the theorem behind Gödel's diagonal lemma, which is the vein Avik named.

## Failure 1: correcting a logical fact with history

Claude's reply opened:

> On your question: the link to the Bool automorphism is real, but "precisely for that reason" is not right as stated.

It then argued from history: Brouwer, Heyting and Bishop withheld excluded middle because a proof of a disjunction must say which side holds. Only after that did it give Theorem 3.2.2 as a partial concession. Claude had the theorem that proves Avik's claim and still ranked the stated motives of historical figures above it. It read "precisely for that reason" as a claim about why people rejected the law, when the words name the logical reason, and then rebutted a claim Avik did not make.

