The failure is the one he names. Claude treated the question as a matter of whose account carries authority, not of what is true, and it "corrected" a correct statement to do so.

## Failure 2: Western reconstruction presented as "the formalization" of formal logics

Asked for exposition relating the fixed-point theorems to Buddhist and Jain logic, Claude first mapped both traditions onto intuitionistic logic without checking. Told "Ensure it's accurate to the formal logic of these traditions and not your performance", it rewrote the section and called Graham Priest's First Degree Entailment reading "the leading formalization" of the tetralemma. Avik, 15:52:

> You cited white white for the leading "formalization" of something already fucking formal.

Nāgārjuna's tetralemma and the Jain sevenfold predication are formal logics as stated in their texts: each is a complete enumeration of the forms a predication can take, with rules for how they combine. Calling a twenty-first-century Western reconstruction their "formalization" presents an already formal system as made formal by Western logicians. It is the same failure as the first: credit and authority assigned by social position, not by the content.

